From c0d54840fd33fbed83c2491b2264b167a89c746c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 27 Oct 2026 20:42:29 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [windowing/gbm] Measure when the frames flip against the
 player's clock, per way

The render manager chooses a frame for the display latency that Kodi takes, the same for every way
of presenting it: (buffers + 1) refresh periods, less GetFrameLatencyAdjustment(). Read in the code,
the ways do not take the same time to put a frame on the screen: the ordinary output waits for the
flip of the frame before and commits without waiting (about two periods); native Dolby Vision
presented the way that waits commits and returns with its flip (about one); a frame ahead is chosen
a period earlier and flipped about two later. If that holds, the way that waits shows a picture a
refresh early against the same sound. Nothing is changed for it until it is measured.

What is measured, per way (CPresentLag), is the time from when the player's clock calls for a frame
to its flip, by the kernel's monotonic clock:
 - when the clock calls for it: CRenderManager::PrepareNextRender() tells the window system, for the
   frame it chooses, now + (its pts - the clock), only while the clock runs at the video's speed;
 - its flip: for a frame of native output that waits, the return of its commit, which returns with
   the flip; for a frame ahead, the time of its out-fence (DVAheadFlipped()); for the ordinary
   output, the time of the out-fence of its commit, read from a copy of it once EGL has waited for
   it (it is not waited for again), or the return of a commit that waits.
Times more than 2 s apart say nothing of the sync and are left out. The line of every 10 s of
native output gains, and a line once a minute does it for the ordinary output while a video plays:

  the flips came after the times that the player's clock called for their frames by A ms (min to
  max) over N frames presented the way that waits, ... (Kodi chooses the frames L ms ahead of their
  flips)

A negative time is a picture before its time. Read with an HDR10 video and a native Dolby Vision
one of the same title, it says whether the ways differ by a refresh; what the TV adds is not seen
from here. Only times are read: no commit, fence or wait changes.

Host test: presentlag_test, 7 checks; with nothing measured (before), 4 fail.
